/**
 * A week of runs, generated by the mock engine — the corpus T4-6 and T4-9 were
 * designed against, and the one their tests count.
 *
 * PROVENANCE, because a fixture nobody can trace is a fixture nobody can trust.
 * Every row below came out of `GET /runs` on a real daemon: real
 * `RunManager`, real runner child (`tsx src/runner-child.ts` with
 * `CW_ENGINE=mock`), real git repo, real migrations. So `jobspec_json`,
 * `report_json` and the state machine are the shapes the daemon actually
 * writes, not shapes anyone imagined.
 *
 * What the mock could NOT produce, and was therefore applied afterwards with
 * direct UPDATEs (the technique `packages/daemon/test/analytics.test.ts`
 * documents at its head):
 *   - costs and turn counts. `MockRunner` reads them from
 *     `steps[].costUsd/turns`, and the only knob `runner-child.ts:125`
 *     exposes is `CW_MOCK_STEP_MS` -> `delayMs`. Every mock run bills $0.
 *   - the failure states, the budget bust, and the run still in flight.
 *   - the two committed branches and their diffstats. The mock writes no
 *     files, so `hasCommitsBeyondBase` is false for every real mock run and
 *     the worktree is pruned.
 *   - timestamps, so the week lands on a fixed Monday morning.
 * Temp paths were rewritten to `/Users/dev/demo-app` and
 * `/Users/dev/.clockwork`; nothing else was edited.
 *
 * The shape of the week, and why each piece is here:
 *   - two recurring jobs — "Nightly deps sweep" (02:00) and "Docs freshness
 *     pass" (03:00), six nights each
 *   - a failure streak — "Flaky e2e triage" failed four nights running
 *   - a budget bust — one `budget_exceeded`, which analytics does NOT count
 *     as `failed` and the inbox's "Failed" chip DOES
 *   - an opencode run, whose `cost_usd` of 0 means "no telemetry", not "free"
 *   - one run still `running` at `NOW` — no `ended_at`, real spend so far
 *   - one finalized run with an F1 plan approval still open
 */
